A GraphQL schema typically has reference cycles (a Post has comments; a Comment has an author; an author has posts...). A query can exploit this with deep nesting:

{
  user(id: 1) {
    friends {
      friends {
        friends {
          friends { name }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

Each level multiplies fanout. A 5-level query with 100 friends per level fetches 10^10 records — a DoS from a single small request. Even without cycles, requesting all fields of all items in a paginated list can be ruinous.

Defenses (layered):

1. Max depth limit: reject any query whose AST depth exceeds N (e.g., 7). Static, simple, cheap.

2. Query complexity / cost analysis: assign each field a cost (a list returning N items costs N*childCost). Sum costs at parse time, reject above a budget. Libraries: graphql-cost-analysis, graphql-validation-complexity.

3. Persisted queries: clients register the queries they intend to send; the server only accepts those by hash. Ad-hoc malicious queries are simply not in the allowlist. Standard for high-scale production deployments.

4. Pagination caps: every list field enforces a max page size at the schema level, not just by convention.

5. Timeouts: every resolver has a hard timeout. A query that escapes static cost analysis still can't run forever.

6. Rate limit by cost, not by request count: charge a cost budget per IP/user; expensive queries consume more.

Don't rely on just one. Static depth limits miss expensive non-deep queries; cost analysis can be miscalibrated; persisted queries are the strongest control but require a build-time pipeline.
